The reduced interaction exponent is `X=N E`, with

`E=(kappa/2) sum_i D_smooth^2(delta_i) + parity term`.

The exact product `S1 x Z2` parity factorization is a continuum statement. At finite q the parity coordinate lives on two shifted q/2 grids; see `RG3_FINITE_Q_SCOPE_CORRECTION.md`.

## Strong TV-to-Haar regime

Under the continuum product-fiber approximation,

`E_Haar[X_smooth] <= A N kappa/(M+1)`, `A=4pi^2 C`.

If `epsilon=A N kappa/(M+1)<1`,

`TV(P_RG3_angles,Haar^M)<=epsilon/(1-epsilon)`.

Thus `N kappa/M ->0` is sufficient for full angular-law TV convergence to Haar.

## Wider geometry-only regime

For geometry, full TV convergence is unnecessary. Jensen gives

`Z>=exp(-E_0[X])`

and therefore for every event B,

`P_G(B)<=exp(E_0[X])P_0(B)`.

The Haar max-gap event obeys

`P_0(maxgap>delta)<=M(1-delta)^(M-1)`.

Hence if

`N kappa=o(M^2)`,

then for every fixed delta>0 the Gibbs probability of a macroscopic phase gap tends to zero. Combined with

`q/M^2 -> infinity`,

phase collisions vanish and RG-3 becomes a dense degree-two polygon on `S1` in probability.

A concrete family with fixed kappa is

`q=M^3`, `N=M^alpha`, `0<alpha<2`.

This is compatible with `N->infinity`, so increasingly sharp single-cell phase localization and diffuse cell geometry can coexist.

## Critical scaling warning

At `N kappa=Theta(M^2)`, the interaction reweighting can be exponential in M and can compete with configuration entropy. No diffuse-geometry theorem is asserted at or above that scale.

